WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:05.499
One dollar and eighty-seven cents. That was all. And sixty cents of it was in pennies.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.584
These pennies were saved by Della, bargaining with the grocer and butcher until she blushed.

3
00:00:10.585 --> 00:00:17.368
Three times a day Della counted it. One dollar and eighty-seven cents. And the next day would be Christmas.

4
00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:21.600
There was clearly nothing to do but flop down on the shabby brown couch.

5
00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:29.624
Della sobbed, but eventually her sobbing subsided. She looked around at their apartment: a furnished flat for eight dollars a week.

6
00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:33.588
It was not exactly a pauper's flat, but it came close.

7
00:00:33.589 --> 00:00:38.548
There was a letterbox into which no letter would go, and a doorbell that made no sound.

8
00:00:38.549 --> 00:00:42.676
On the door was a sign, "Mr. James Dillingham Young."

9
00:00:42.677 --> 00:00:52.035
But whenever Mr. James Dillingham Young came home, he was called Jim and hugged by Mrs. James Dillingham Young, already introduced to you as Della.

10
00:00:52.036 --> 00:00:59.215
Della finished crying. Tomorrow would be Christmas Day and she only had a dollar and eighty-seven cents with which to buy Jim a present.

11
00:00:59.216 --> 00:01:02.047
She had been saving every penny she could for months.

12
00:01:02.048 --> 00:01:07.721
Twenty dollars a week doesn't go far, and living expenses had been greater than she had calculated.

13
00:01:07.722 --> 00:01:12.702
Della stared at herself in the long glass windows. Suddenly she whirled around.

14
00:01:12.703 --> 00:01:18.987
Her eyes were shining but her face had lost its color in twenty seconds. Rapidly she let down her hair.

15
00:01:18.988 --> 00:01:25.041
Della's beautiful hair rippled and shined like a cascade of brown waters. It reached below her knee.

16
00:01:25.042 --> 00:01:29.337
Della had an idea. Maybe she could afford a Christmas present . . .

17
00:01:29.338 --> 00:01:33.630
Now there were two things that Jim and Della Dillingham Young adored.

18
00:01:33.631 --> 00:01:40.367
One was Jim's gold watch that had been his father's and his grandfather's. The other was Della's hair.

19
00:01:40.368 --> 00:01:46.766
Della put her hair up quickly and nervously. She put on her old brown jacket and her old brown hat.

20
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:54.209
With a whirl of her skirt and the brilliant sparkle in her eyes, she fluttered out the door and down the stairs to the street.

21
00:01:54.210 --> 00:02:02.412
Della walked downtown and stopped outside a shop whose sign said, "Mrs. Sofronie. Hair Goods of All Kinds."

22
00:02:02.413 --> 00:02:09.614
She took a deep breath and went inside. "Will you buy my hair?" Della asked a large lady at the counter.

23
00:02:09.615 --> 00:02:14.482
"Take off your hat and let's have a look at it," the lady said.

24
00:02:14.483 --> 00:02:19.114
She was Mrs. Sofronie, the shopkeeper. The cascade rippled down.

25
00:02:19.115 --> 00:02:24.717
"Twenty dollars," said Mrs. Sofronie, lifting the mass of hair with a practiced hand.

26
00:02:24.718 --> 00:02:27.531
"Give it to me quick," said Della.

27
00:02:27.531 --> 00:02:31.144
Della spent the next two hours looking in stores for Jim's present.

28
00:02:31.145 --> 00:02:35.287
She found it at last. It surely had been made for Jim and no one else.

29
00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:40.161
There was nothing like it in any of the stores, and she had turned all of them inside out.

30
00:02:40.162 --> 00:02:43.327
It was a platinum fob chain with a simple design.

31
00:02:43.328 --> 00:02:48.951
It was not gaudy or too ornamental, but its simplicity and style demonstrated its quality.

32
00:02:48.952 --> 00:02:52.856
As soon as she saw it, she knew that it must be Jim's.

33
00:02:52.857 --> 00:02:57.505
Twenty-one dollars it cost, and she hurried home with the eighty-seven cents.

34
00:02:57.506 --> 00:03:02.041
With the fob chain on his watch, Jim could check the time in anyone's company.

35
00:03:02.042 --> 00:03:10.327
Grand as the watch was, sometimes Jim checked it secretly because he was ashamed of the old leather strap that he used in the place of a chain.

36
00:03:10.328 --> 00:03:17.318
When Della reached home, she got out her curling irons, lit the gas and went to work on her now very short hair.

37
00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:22.925
Within forty minutes her head was covered with tiny, close-lying curls that made her look like a schoolboy.

38
00:03:22.926 --> 00:03:26.271
She looked at her reflection in the mirror carefully and critically.

39
00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:35.002
"Jim will say I look like a Coney Island chorus girl! But what could I do? What could I buy with a dollar and eighty-seven cents?"

40
00:03:35.003 --> 00:03:41.132
At seven o'clock the coffee was made and the frying-pan was on the back of the stove and ready to cook the chops.

41
00:03:41.133 --> 00:03:48.637
Jim was never late. Della doubled the fob chain in her hand and sat on the corner of the table near the door that he always entered.

42
00:03:48.638 --> 00:03:52.742
Then Della heard Jim's steps on the stairs. She turned white for a moment.

43
00:03:52.743 --> 00:03:57.361
Della had a habit of saying a little silent prayer about the simplest everyday things.

44
00:03:57.362 --> 00:04:02.104
Now she whispered, "Please, God, make him think that I am still pretty."

45
00:04:02.105 --> 00:04:09.545
The door opened, and Jim stepped in and closed it. He looked thin and serious. Poor fellow, he was only twenty-two.

46
00:04:09.546 --> 00:04:12.478
He needed a new overcoat and he had no gloves.

47
00:04:12.479 --> 00:04:18.613
Jim's eyes fixed upon Della, and there was an expression in them she could not read, and it terrified her.

48
00:04:18.614 --> 00:04:25.371
It was not anger, nor surprise, nor disapproval, nor horror, nor any of the sentiments she had been prepared for.

49
00:04:25.372 --> 00:04:29.030
He simply stared at her with a peculiar expression on his face.

50
00:04:29.031 --> 00:04:33.631
Della jumped off the table and went to him. "Jim, darling," she cried.

51
00:04:33.632 --> 00:04:40.631
"Don't look at me that way. I cut my hair off because I couldn't have lived through another Christmas without giving you a present.

52
00:04:40.632 --> 00:04:47.365
It will grow again. You don't mind, do you? Say Merry Christmas, Jim, and let's be happy!"

53
00:04:47.366 --> 00:04:54.605
Jim's strange expression had not changed. Della pleaded with him, "Come on, Jim. I bought you such a beautiful gift."

54
00:04:54.606 --> 00:05:01.628
"You've cut off your hair," said Jim slowly, as if it was taking him a long time to understand this simple fact.

55
00:05:01.629 --> 00:05:06.593
"I cut it off and sold it. But I'm still the same without my hair!"

56
00:05:06.594 --> 00:05:09.936
"Where do you say your hair is gone?" he said.

57
00:05:09.937 --> 00:05:12.034
"I told you, I sold it."

58
00:05:12.035 --> 00:05:21.100
Jim suddenly blinked his eyes and shook his head. Then he drew a package from his overcoat pocket and threw it upon the table.

59
00:05:21.101 --> 00:05:26.950
"Don't make any mistake, Dell," Jim said. "There's no way I can love you any less.

60
00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:30.481
No haircut or shampoo can change the way I feel about you.

61
00:05:30.482 --> 00:05:35.472
But if you unwrap that package you'll see why I've been acting strange these past five minutes."

62
00:05:35.473 --> 00:05:42.589
Dell's nimble fingers tore at the string and paper. There was an ecstatic scream of joy and then there was a wailing of tears.

63
00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:53.040
Upon the table there was a set of hair combs─beautiful combs made from real tortoise shells with jewels on the rims─just the shade to wear in her nonexistent hair.

64
00:05:53.041 --> 00:05:59.617
Dell knew they were expensive hair combs. She had wished for them but thought she would never own such beautiful things.

65
00:05:59.618 --> 00:06:03.504
And now they were hers, but she had no hair to wear them in!

66
00:06:03.505 --> 00:06:11.501
But she hugged them to her chest, and she looked up with a smile and said, "My hair grows so fast, Jim!"

67
00:06:11.502 --> 00:06:18.023
Jim had not yet seen his beautiful present. Della held out the fob chain eagerly upon her open palm.

68
00:06:18.024 --> 00:06:22.242
"Isn't it smart, Jim? I hunted all over town to find it.

69
00:06:22.243 --> 00:06:26.841
You'll have to check the time a hundred times a day now. Give me your watch.

70
00:06:26.842 --> 00:06:33.522
I want to see how it looks. I know it will look a hundred times nicer than that old leather strap!"

71
00:06:33.523 --> 00:06:40.205
Instead of obeying, Jim flopped down onto the couch and put his hands under the back of his head and smiled.

72
00:06:40.206 --> 00:06:46.521
"Della," he said, "let's put our Christmas presents away and keep them for a while.

73
00:06:46.522 --> 00:06:54.212
They're too nice to use just now. I sold the watch to get the money to buy your combs. Let's have some dinner."

74
00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:59.217
The Magi were three wise men who brought valuable gifts to baby Jesus in the manger.

75
00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:03.094
Perhaps the Magi were the first people to ever give Christmas presents.

76
00:07:03.095 --> 00:07:06.573
In this story, Christmas gifts were very important.

77
00:07:06.574 --> 00:07:14.880
Della and Jim loved each other so much that they were willing to sacrifice the greatest treasures in their house─Della's hair and Jim's watch.

78
00:07:14.881 --> 00:07:18.388
They didn't have a lot of money, but they had a lot of love.

79
00:07:18.389 --> 00:07:25.984
Perhaps they were foolish, but Della and Jim understood about special gifts and could enjoy Christmas together.

